Title:
Red Team Tactics: Utilizing Syscalls in C# (Part 2) – Writing The Code

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post walks through building a C# proof-of-concept that executes a direct Windows syscall (NtCreateFile) from .NET without calling the ntdll export, aiming to bypass user-mode API hooks.  
- It shows how to structure a project into separate classes for syscall stubs, native interop definitions, and the main program logic to keep the code modular for future expansion.  
- The technique extracts the syscall stub bytes (via WinDBG disassembly), embeds them as a byte array, pins the buffer, marks it executable with VirtualProtect, and invokes it via Marshal.GetDelegateForFunctionPointer.  
- It covers the required P/Invoke structs/enums (e.g., OBJECT_ATTRIBUTES, IO_STATUS_BLOCK, ACCESS_MASK) and Unicode path initialization (RtlUnicodeStringInit) to correctly marshal parameters.  
- Validation is demonstrated using ProcMon call stacks showing the operation reaching ntoskrnl.exe without ntdll frames, indicating a direct syscall path.  
- Useful for red teamers, implant/tool developers, and researchers exploring syscall-based evasion and .NET unmanaged interop.

Technical Focus:
- Direct syscalls from C# via embedded syscall stubs (shellcode-like byte arrays)
- Unsafe code, fixed/pinning, and CLR/GC interaction
- Delegate-based invocation (Marshal.GetDelegateForFunctionPointer, UnmanagedFunctionPointer)
- Memory protection changes with VirtualProtect (RWX)
- P/Invoke of NT native structures/enums (OBJECT_ATTRIBUTES, IO_STATUS_BLOCK, NTSTATUS)
- Syscall stub extraction and syscall ID identification using WinDBG/ntdll disassembly

Use Cases:
- Building syscall-based .NET tradecraft to reduce exposure to user-mode API hooking
- Creating modular syscall libraries for integration into other C# tooling (e.g., dumping/injection utilities)
- Researching EDR evasion boundaries between user-mode hooks and kernel transitions
- Learning correct marshaling of NT APIs and native data structures in managed code

Keywords:
C#, .NET Framework 3.5, Windows syscalls, NtCreateFile, ntdll, ntoskrnl.exe, WinDBG, disassembly, syscall ID, P/Invoke, Marshal.GetDelegateForFunctionPointer, UnmanagedFunctionPointer, unsafe, fixed, VirtualProtect, PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE, OBJECT_ATTRIBUTES, IO_STATUS_BLOCK, UNICODE_STRING, RtlUnicodeStringInit, ProcMon call stack, API hooking bypass, SharpCall